Reduction, and reduce.

Sum, count, max, and min all do the same fundamental thing: they walk a stream and fold it down to one value. That pattern is called reduction, and reduce is the general tool underneath all of them — the operation you reach for when the named shortcut you want doesn't exist.

Let's look at the shape of every reduction. It repeatedly combines a running accumulator with the next element, using a two-argument function. You start with a value, and then for each element, you compute "the accumulator so far, combined with this element", giving a new accumulator. Sum is the archetype. You start total at zero, and for each number, total becomes total plus that number. That starting value is the identity, and the combine step is a function from accumulator and element to a new accumulator.

Reduce is exactly that loop, expressed as a value. You give it two things: an identity — the starting accumulator — and a binary operator — the combine step, from accumulator and element to new accumulator. So total equals nums dot stream dot reduce, of zero, and the function accumulator-plus-n. Read it as: start at zero, and fold each number in with plus. There's a shorthand too — reduce of zero and integer-dot-sum. And here's the beauty: swap the identity and the operator, and you get a different reduction from the very same machinery. Reduce of one, times, is product. Reduce of empty-string, concat, is concatenation.

Now, two rules the identity and combiner must obey — and these matter enormously once section eight runs them in parallel. First, the identity must be neutral: combining it with any element must return that element unchanged. Zero plus x equals x. One times x equals x. Empty-string plus x equals x. So use zero for a sum, one for a product — and never the other way around. Second, the operator must be associative: a-combined-with-b, then with c, must equal a combined with, b-combined-with-c. Addition and multiplication qualify. Subtraction does not — ten minus three, then minus two, is not the same as ten minus, three-minus-two. Associativity is what lets Java split the work across threads and recombine in any order. It's the hidden contract behind parallel streams.

Now compare the two-argument form with the one-argument form. The form we just saw always returns a plain value, because the identity is the answer for an empty stream — an empty sum is zero. But drop the identity, and you get a different, more honest signature. Reduce with just the operator, and no identity, returns an optional. With no starting value, an empty stream has no result at all — so this overload returns an optional, which is empty when the stream was empty. It's the same emptiness honesty you saw in min and max — which are themselves just reductions.

So when should you actually use reduce? Most of the time, you shouldn't. Prefer the named terminal that says what you mean — sum, count, max, average are all reductions with a readable name. And for building collections, collect, in the next section, is the right tool, not reduce. Save raw reduce for a genuinely custom fold with no built-in equivalent: combining records into a running summary, multiplying a stream of factors, merging with a domain-specific operator. When the shape is "many values in, one value out, by a combining rule", and nothing named fits — reduce is the primitive that expresses it.
